lessons.content:
Spring Boot simplifies building web applications and REST APIs using Spring MVC.

@RestController marks a class as a controller whose handler methods return response bodies by default.

@RequestMapping defines a common URL prefix or additional request-matching conditions.

@GetMapping, @PostMapping, @PutMapping, @PatchMapping and @DeleteMapping map HTTP methods to Java methods.

@PathVariable reads values embedded in a URL path.

@RequestParam reads query parameters, such as page=0 or search=java.

@RequestBody tells Spring to deserialize a request body, commonly JSON, into a Java object.

ResponseEntity allows a controller to explicitly specify the HTTP status, headers and response body.

A typical Spring Boot REST application uses spring-boot-starter-web and includes a class annotated with @SpringBootApplication.

Controllers should focus on handling HTTP requests and responses. Business rules belong in a service layer, while persistence operations belong in a repository layer.

A simple REST endpoint can be tested from a browser for GET requests or with an HTTP client for other methods.

